 almost as soon as it  began over the weekend  hundreds of soldiers and civilians have  died in the fighting over the disputed  region  of nagorno-karabakh and as redmond  shannon reports  there are now fears the conflict could  escalate and draw in regional powers  like turkey and russia  orphaned by war these azerbaijani  siblings lead a funeral procession  for their parents they were among nine  people killed according to azerbaijan  by armenian bombing coming shortly after  a ceasefire brokered by russia  over the weekend what we see here is  just armenia's purpose to kill  civilians once again shows a true face  of armenia  armenia says the accusation it targeted  civilians is an  absolute lie the problem might be that  azerbaijan  very often um resorts to well  they choose to locate their military  positions in uh  close to residential areas i don't know  but we never we never target  a peaceful population armenia says it  was azerbaijan  backed by turkish military support that  broke the ceasefire minutes  after it came into effect and if turkey  previously has been a kind of behind the  scenes  player in azerbaijan's domestic and  foreign policy now they're really  hands-on  turkey has denied numerous reports it's  recruited syrian mercenaries to fight  alongside azerbaijani forces  nagorno-karabakh is part of an  armenian-controlled region which is  internationally recognized as part of  azerbaijan  after the two countries became  independent war in the 1990s killed  tens of thousands of people until a  delicate truce was reached  analysts believe years of neglect by  foreign powers who oversaw the deal  have led to the renewed hostilities  armenia's foreign minister was in moscow  to meet his russian counterpart on  monday  he also pointed to a lack of independent  monitoring of the region  russia's foreign minister dismissed  calls for turkey to be more involved in  brokering a peace deal  last week canada suspended some arms  exports to turkey over allegations  equipment was being passed on to  azerbaijani forces  canadian foreign minister francois  philippe champagne is in europe this  week  and will discuss the conflict with nato  and eu leaders  redmond shannon global news london  still ahead how an adoption agency's  financial fumbles cost the dreams of  some prospective parents  adopting a child comes with many hurdles  security checks  parenting courses a huge financial cost  and  there are no guarantees a number of bc  families found that out the hard way  paying thousands to an agency they had  assumed was being overseen by the  provincial government  but it suddenly shut down in 2019 and as  robin gill reports  one prospective parents detective work  uncovered problems that went back  years there are children in this world  who need a home and there are families  like corey bell and her husband  who want to adopt i have like no  specific desire to have biological  children so i always thought i'd do it  the 38 year old turned to choices  adoption a victoria charity  she spent twelve thousand dollars to  have social workers  interview her and take courses on taking  on a child  that isn't hers biologically then the  agency suddenly closed last year  with no recourse for belle who was among  90 families  left in the lurch it has been very  devastating  i mean it's it's  a hard enough thing adopting it's  a lot you do background checks and  classes and  you wait but to feel like you aren't  haven't been supported  by your government like you've been  abandoned  by a charity organization it's  devastating  before choices shut down it asked and  got  90 000 from the province to pay its  debts  bell uncovered documents she feels  should have been red flags  and filed a complaint with the  provincial ombuds person  the financial documents that we got a  hold of do  show a lot of very unnecessary travel a  lot of very expensive coffees  there was a very fancy 25th anniversary  party  that was paid for by adoptive parents  not by the government  not by charity donations it was paid for  by me  but there was never an audit of any of  these expenses  when the troubles of this adoption  agency became apparent  the ministry in charge of adoptions in  this province said it would step  in but it's been close to a year and the  families  are still waiting bc is in the midst of  a provincial election so no one from the  government will comment  some families turn to victoria police to  file a complaint citing financial  mismanagement and fraud  the police department couldn't confirm  if there's an investigation  the families were told they could have  their files transferred to two other  agencies that still  operate in british columbia i would  still love to adopt but i don't see how  it's possible  in this scheme i can't just keep  throwing money down a hole  but it means starting all over again and  the emotional burden  has already been too much robin gill  global news  victoria up next wyatt vancouver man  is now king of the ring in the eyes of a  hollywood star  american actor john cryer has something  pretty special to be grateful for this  canadian thanksgiving  the star known for his role in the  sitcom two and a half men  is working in vancouver he was walking  along the city's sea wall when he  dropped his wedding ring  after a futile search he called in a  local man who helps people recover lost  jewelry  and then this happened how does this  feel  oh no oh you're kidding me  there you go beautiful man that is  absolutely beautiful  wow thank you that is the one now wait  we have to check it out i have no doubt  boom  took to twitter with his story in a  lengthy post that's been shared nearly  ten  thousand times he gave thanks to this  guy  saying i am still beside myself this is  chris  he is awesome i got lucky uh  probably three minutes into the search i  found it  in the middle of the grass in a  three-foot area  his reaction it was like most people's  kind of disbelief like  and and shock and surprise and he was  happy  and that's global national for this  monday i'm sophie louis we leave you  tonight with a heartfelt tribute to a  beloved hockey coach  darcy hogan was head coach of the humble  broncos he was killed in the 2018 bus  crash that also took the lives of 15  others  now he's being honored with a new bronze  statue in his hometown of peace river  alberta  thanks for watching and have a great  night happy thanksgiving  [Music]
 

